@Jay Zeng @Lindani Phiri I spoke with Florencio a bit on this to clarify how much needs to be in scope when we talk about "zero trust" and "trust zones." It is a matter of degree. For example, validating signed, short-lived bearer tokens are certainly less trusting than unsigned ones, but that still means you trust the "bearer" of the token, which could still technically be a bad actor. The defense against this is the fact that these expire. A stronger defense would be something like mTLS everywhere, or DPoP tokens, or HTTP request signatures. As I understand it that is really closer to "zero trust" but not necessary at this time
